data.content:
Here's what breaks in EF Core 10 and how to fix each piece:

---

## 1. `ExecuteUpdateAsync` — expression tree no longer compiles

**What changed:** `ExecuteUpdateAsync` now accepts a regular `Func<...>` instead of `Expression<Func<...>>`. Your manual expression tree code won't compile.

**Fix — replace with simple imperative code:**

```csharp
await context.Blogs.ExecuteUpdateAsync(s =>
{
    s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, 8);
    if (nameChanged)
    {
        s.SetProperty(b => b.Name, "foo");
    }
});
```

No more `Expression.Lambda`/`Expression.Call` needed.

---

## 2. Complex types — column name uniquification + nested path changes

**Two changes:**

| Change | Effect |
|--------|--------|
| Duplicate column names are now auto-uniquified | `ShippingAddress.Street` and `BillingAddress.Street` will get different column names (e.g. `Street`, `Street1`) instead of silently sharing one column. |
| Nested complex types use full path | `Order.Payment.BillingAddress.City` now maps to `Payment_BillingAddress_City` instead of `BillingAddress_City`. |

**Fix — pin existing column names explicitly if you have an existing database:**

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, p =>
        p.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("Street"));
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, p =>
        p.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("Street"));
});

// For nested complex types, pin old names:
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>()
    .ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment)
    .ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress)
    .Property(a => a.City)
    .HasColumnName("BillingAddress_City");
```

Or let EF generate a new migration and apply the column renames to your DB.

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `logCommandText` parameter

**What changed:** Methods like `CommandReaderExecuting` now require an additional `string logCommandText` parameter (contains potentially-redacted SQL for logging).

**Fix:**

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        string logCommandText)   // ← add this parameter
    {
        // Use logCommandText for logging (redacted)
        // Use command.CommandText for actual SQL
    }
}
```

Update all other interface methods (`CommandReaderExecuted`, `CommandScalarExecuting`, etc.) the same way.
